  1. unstakeAndExitPool → _exitPool runs in the vault's storage context (the lib is reached via _delegateCall), so
  address(this) is the vault. The Curve V2 calls remove_liquidity_one_coin(..., true, address(this)) and
  remove_liquidity(..., true, address(this)) therefore instruct Curve to push native ETH to the vault itself.
  2. That is the whole of the "reentrancy window": Curve performs a value transfer whose recipient is the vault. The
  only code that executes during that transfer is the vault's own receive() — a plain payable sink. No attacker-supplied
  address is ever passed as the receiver, and neither TOKEN_1 nor TOKEN_2 is attacker-controlled (both are immutables
  read from ICurvePool.coins() at construction). There is no callback, no hook, no onERC777-style handoff, and nothing
  in this path that hands execution to an unprivileged third party's contract.
  3. A reentrancy finding requires an attacker-controlled frame to execute while protocol state is mid-flight. Here the
  only frame that executes is the vault's empty receive(), which immediately returns to Curve, which returns to
  _exitPool. The attacker cannot make the money market read convertToAssets from inside that frame, because the attacker
  has no frame — the ETH lands in the vault, not in a contract the attacker wrote. The claim's premise ("Curve pushes
  native ETH into the vault's receive() … giving a permissionless reentrancy window") confuses the vault receiving ETH
  with the attacker gaining control flow; those are not the same thing, and only the former happens.
  4. The _checkReentrancyContext() / checkReentrancyContext() machinery is not even on this path — it is a separate
  external (non-delegate) call used to assert Curve's own lock is free before pricing, and it is what makes the lib's
  receive() necessary (there address(this) is the lib). Its scope is irrelevant to _exitPool, so "only guards Curve's
  own lock" does not establish an exploit in _exitPool.
  5. Independently, Curve V2's remove_liquidity/remove_liquidity_one_coin are @nonreentrant('lock'), so even if some
  frame did execute during the ETH push, re-entering the pool (the thing the escrow/convertToAssets pricing path would
  touch) reverts.

  No step exists at which an unprivileged external attacker obtains execution during _exitPool. The claim is not backed
  by the actual control flow.

